{"name":"Tobacco Leaf","hex":"#8c724f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tobacco","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Nicotiana.","Leaves of Nicotiana tabacum and some other species cultivated and harvested to make cigarettes, cigars, snuff, for smoking in pipes or for chewing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To indulge in tobacco; to smoke.","To treat with tobacco."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tobacco"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tobacco","blocks":[{"text":"Tobacco is the common name of several plants in the genus Nicotiana of the family Solanaceae, and the general term for any product prepared from the cured leaves of these plants. Seventy-nine species of tobacco are known, but the chief commercial crop is N. tabacum. The more potent variant N. rustica is also used in some countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Ripe_tobacco_leaf_3037.JPG/330px-Ripe_tobacco_leaf_3037.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobacco"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg/500px-DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg/1280px-DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg/500px-DunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADunhillEarlyMorningPipeMurrays.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg/500px-A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg/1280px-A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg/500px-A_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tobacco drying kiln in Myrtleford, Victoria, Australia, 2018. This kiln was built in 1957, and moved to Rotary Park in 2000. Kilns of this design were built from the early 1930s through to the late 1960s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_traditional_tobacco_drying_kiln_in_Myrtleford%2C_Victoria%2C_Australia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg/500px-Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg/1280px-Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg/500px-Basma-tobacco-drying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Basma tobacco leaves drying in the sun at Pomak village in Xanthi, Greece","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasma-tobacco-drying.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Fumo_Mercado_Minas_Gerais.jpg/500px-Fumo_Mercado_Minas_Gerais.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Fumo_Mercado_Minas_Gerais.jpg/1280px-Fumo_Mercado_Minas_Gerais.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb